I was the trial judge when Chris Young  went to trial in 2012 that case it was  in front of me I had just taken the  bench about a year earlier and and Chris  was an early trial ultimately I did have  to send him to life in prison it was it  was mandatory my hands were tied  normally a judge has discretion to  fashion a sentence that is sufficient  but not more harsh than necessary to  serve the purposes of why we send people  to prison in his case it was a mandatory  sentence it was gut-wrenching to do that  and to know that there was nothing that  I could do this was just a formality and  as Chris spoke before I sentence him  he's talking about all the things that  he would do is if someday he's going to  get out but we all know that you're not  going anywhere except to the Bureau of  Prisons and so it was it was  gut-wrenching and I think that's part of  it I think about him a lot he's going to  get a chance to speak but I kept feeling  keep talking because the longer you talk  then I don't have to say life it was it  was horrible I had been on the bench for  about six years coming right at six  years and part of what I saw was this  criminal justice system that I thought  was unfair in many ways and particularly  with mandatory minimum sentencing and so  the decision became am i more useful  sitting on the bench which would have  been very easy to do and live out my  life it's a lifetime appointment or is  I'm better serving the public in this  country by stepping down and talking  about these things and working to change  the system and the system can be changed  it's going to take political will but  the mandatory minimums need to be  reduced if not eliminated Chris Young  should be released because we've got an  individual who's now 30 he's done eight  years in prison now  who has served his time he's been  punished for his crime and keeping him  imprisoned longer serves no purpose  other than revenge you know I don't know  what purpose it would serve and so  you've got a young man who really has  the ability to add something back to  society I think the takeaway message  from this is that we are a nation of  second chances people deserve second  chances and we need to find a way to  look and find them and get that second  chance to the people who deserve it and  Chris Young really illustrates that  these kind of sentences are not who we  are as a nation it's not justice for the  person who's sentenced and it's not it's  not justice for the rest of us this  country can do better than that
 

